---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { loadMayHeadline, loadGeHeadline } from "../lib/siteData";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";

const may = loadMayHeadline();
const ge = loadGeHeadline();
// The forward record. A backtest is a replay of a model against an election it
// was built after; this is what happened when the model was published in
// advance and then graded. The second is the more honest headline, so it leads.
let postaudit: any = null;
try {
  postaudit = JSON.parse(
    readFileSync(path.join(process.cwd(), "data/transparency/may-2026-postaudit-2026-05-10.json"), "utf8"),
  );
} catch { postaudit = null; }
const prereg = postaudit?.prereg?.summary || null;
const live = postaudit?.live?.summary || null;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How accurate are the forecasts?"
  description="Every forecast on UK Elections is replayed against the most recent real election. The full results, including every miss, are published here."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Accuracy record</p>
    <h1>How accurate are these forecasts?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Before any forecast on this site is published, the same model is run against the most recent real
      election to see how well it would have done. The full results live here, including every category
      where it was wrong.
    </p>
  </section>

  {prereg && live && (
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The forward record</p>
        <h2>What happened when we forecast a real election in advance</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        On 26 April 2026 we published a forecast for every ward voting on 7 May and recorded its hash so it
        could not be quietly revised. Then the votes were counted. This is the result, and it is the number
        that matters most on this page, because everything below it is a replay.
      </p>
      <div class="accuracy-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="big-number">{(prereg.winner_accuracy * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span>
          <p class="big-number-label">winners called correctly across {prereg.ballots_evaluated.toLocaleString()} wards, from the forecast locked 11 days before polling day</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="big-number">{(prereg.major_party_mae_avg * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</span>
          <p class="big-number-label">average gap between predicted and actual party share, same locked forecast</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="big-number">{(live.winner_accuracy * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span>
          <p class="big-number-label">winners called correctly by the forecast as users actually saw it on polling day, after a late Reform realignment adjustment</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="big-number">{(live.major_party_mae_avg * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</span>
          <p class="big-number-label">average share gap for the forecast as served</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        Half the wards called wrong is not a good number and we are not going to dress it up. The full
        breakdown, what we changed because of it, and what our confidence label turned out to be worth are
        on <a href="/past-results/">past results</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Backtests, which are replays</p>
      <h2>How the model scores against elections it did not forecast live</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      These are replays: the model run against a past election to see how it would have done. They flatter
      any model relative to a real forward forecast, which is why the locked May 2026 result above leads
      this page rather than these.
    </p>
    <div class="accuracy-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="big-number">{(may.backtest_winner_accuracy * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span>
        <p class="big-number-label">winners called correctly across {may.ward_count.toLocaleString()} wards in the May 2024 local elections</p>
        <p class="muted-row"><a href="/data-quality/2024-backtest/">See the full local backtest →</a></p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="big-number">{(may.backtest_major_party_mae * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</span>
        <p class="big-number-label">average gap between predicted and actual party share, May 2024 local backtest</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="big-number">{(ge.backtest_winner_accuracy * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span>
        <p class="big-number-label">winners called correctly across 649 constituencies in the 2024 general election</p>
        <p class="muted-row"><a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/">See the full GE backtest →</a></p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="big-number">{(ge.backtest_major_party_mae * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</span>
        <p class="big-number-label">average gap between predicted and actual party share, 2024 general election backtest</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Honesty rules</p>
      <h2>Things we won't do</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="numbered-list">
      <li><strong>We don't fit on the test set.</strong> The 2024 backtests use only what a forecaster running the model in May 2024 would have known, no peeking at the actual results during model fit.</li>
      <li><strong>We don't quietly retire models that did badly.</strong> The same model code that generated the backtest also generates the live forecast. If we change the model, the backtest re-runs automatically and the new score is published, even if it's worse.</li>
      <li><strong>We publish the worst misses, not just the headline.</strong> Every backtest page lists the wards or constituencies where the model was furthest off, with no cherry-picking.</li>
      <li><strong>We pre-register the live forecast before each election.</strong> The sha256 of every prediction file is recorded ahead of time so we can prove there's been no after-the-fact tampering. See <a href="/transparency/">Transparency</a>.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">For comparison</p>
      <h2>How we sit against published forecasters</h2>
    </div>
    <table class="seat-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Forecaster</th><th>Method</th><th>Winner accuracy on GE2024</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>YouGov 2024 (fared paper)</td><td>150k in-campaign panel; full Bayesian MRP</td><td>~92%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stonehaven 2024</td><td>Bayesian MRP</td><td>~94%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Focaldata 2024</td><td>MRP with regradient</td><td>~87%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Survation 2024</td><td>MRP, no unwinding</td><td>~84%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>UK Elections (this site)</strong></td><td>STM swing + per-constituency BES priors + tactical voting + demographic floors and ceilings, no panel fit</td><td><strong>{(ge.backtest_winner_accuracy * 100).toFixed(1)}%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Uniform National Swing baseline</td><td>The simplest non-trivial model</td><td>~71.8%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="caption">
      The gap to Survation/Focaldata is essentially the in-campaign panel-fitted MRP step that fresh fieldwork
      enables. We don't run a fieldwork panel, so we won't catch up to that fully, but we're <strong>5 percentage points</strong>
      ahead of Uniform National Swing, the standard baseline for forecasters without a panel.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
